  "_commit": {
    "id": "fb020d07add779064b66e17f985bfacc849f37b0",
    "author": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "committer": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "message": "docs: correct benchmark numbers with density-aware fixtures\n\nAll benchmark fixtures for the .marginVars() and .check_gram_size() perf\nentries on this branch were written via writeCoordArray() under\nDelayedArray's dense-budgeted default block settings, not the\ndensity-aware setAutoBlockSize(target_bytes / density) +\nsetAutoBlockShape(\"scale\") correction this package's own writeCoordArray()\ndocs recommend for sparse arrays. Re-measured both with correctly\nconfigured fixtures, and against deliberately uncorrected fixtures of the\nsame data, to check sensitivity to the resulting Parquet partition/\nrow-group layout directly.\n\n.marginVars(): 1.6x-1.7x on the original 2000 x 300, ~60%-dense fixture\n(vs. the previously reported 1.57x) and 1.8x-1.85x on a larger, more\nrealistic single-cell-like 20000 x 5000, 2%-dense fixture. The smaller\nfixture writes to a single partition regardless of block settings (too\nsmall to trigger the distortion); the larger one does differ materially\n(1 file/5 row groups corrected vs. 4 files/8 row groups uncorrected), yet\nthe measured speedup is the same within run-to-run noise under both\nlayouts, consistent with the fix's benefit coming from eliminating a\nredundant full-table scan rather than per-row throughput.\n\n.check_gram_size(): 580x-840x across repeated runs (vs. the previously\nreported 205x from a single run). This fix is structural (skips an entire\nquery execution: 1 query -> 0 queries), so it was not expected to be\nsensitive to block/chunk layout, and measurement confirms this: corrected\nvs. uncorrected fixture writes changed the number only from 813x to 839x,\nwithin noise, while ambient DuckDB thread count/machine load (unrelated to\nblock configuration) moved it between 580x and 840x across sessions. 205x\nsits below that range but is the same phenomenon, not an effect of the\nblock-shape methodology gap.\n",
    "time": 1793900196
  },
